  "title": "Unified Framework for Personality and Cognitive Control",
  "summary": "This paper investigates whether personality can be represented and controlled as a set of cognitive processes using the eight Jungian Cognitive Functions, introducing a framework comprising a Jungian evaluation protocol and a dataset of over 2,100 role-playing character narrations. The study demonstrates that activation steering enables effective control over all cognitive functions through monotonic steering, with findings highlighting the geometric structure of steering vectors and their impact on both control and safety.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "1. Activation steering enables control and interpretation of LLMs, yet existing work primarily models personality through static trait frameworks.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "2. The study introduces a framework for representing personality as a set of cognitive processes using the eight Jungian Cognitive Functions.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "3. The activation steering vector extraction and evaluation experiments show effective monotonic control over all eight cognitive functions.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
  ],
